    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Gyleen Formation sandstone of southern Ireland has undergone mechanical and chemical deformation evidenced by pressure solution seams (PSSs). These are complex planar structures which form a hierarchy of linked PSS segments that can be observed from the grain-scale to the outcrop-scale: (1) The seams initiate as poorly-organized “inter-granular PSSs” at quartz grain to grain. Clay and mica form the core of the seam, (2) Thickening of the seams and lateral growth by linkage of adjescent segments in consistent orientations produces “incipient single PSSs” with further dissolution of quartz and accumulation of the residual clays and micas. The residue forms the ‘core’ of the pressure solution seam and it grows both laterally and transversely by linkage of neighboring seams and the advanced dissolution of quarts respectively. (3) Multiple incipient single PSSs can concentrate in thin tabular zones that appear as single “macroscopic PSSs”. They may coalesce by lateral or transverse linkage to lengthen and thicken the seams as they do at the grain-scale. The length and spacing of PSSs have log normal distributions at outcrop, hand sample and thin section scale suggesting that both length and spacing are scale independent.\r\n\r\nThe mechanism of pressure solution is complex as PSSs are associated with permanent strain, can bear load and can link through transverse coalescence, unlike opening mode joints. Due to this, the evolution of spacing distributions of solution seams with strain may not fully follow the distribution path associated with joints.",
